\title{The Order Theory of Abstract Argumentation: The Fundamental Lemma
and the Identity of Preferred and Maximal Complete Extensions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the maximal-extension theory of Dung-style abstract
argumentation frameworks with no finiteness and no well-foundedness
hypothesis on the attack relation. From the classical definitions of
conflict-freedom, defense, admissibility, completeness, stability, and
preferredness, we isolate a single structural engine---the
\emph{Fundamental Lemma}---asserting that an admissible set stays
admissible when any argument it defends is added. From this we derive,
uniformly and unconditionally, that every preferred (maximal admissible)
extension is complete; that every stable extension is complete and indeed
preferred; that unions of chains of admissible sets are admissible; and
hence, via Zorn's lemma, that every admissible set extends to a preferred
extension, so preferred extensions always exist. These combine into a
clean structural characterization: a set is preferred if and only if it is
a maximal complete extension. Together with the companion theory of the
grounded (least complete) extension, this organizes the complete
extensions of any framework into a \emph{pointed poset} whose least
element is the grounded extension and whose maximal elements are exactly
the preferred extensions.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Dung's theory of abstract argumentation reduces defeasible reasoning to
its combinatorial core: one fixes a set of \emph{arguments} and a binary
\emph{attack relation}, discards the internal content of each argument,
and studies which sets can be rationally accepted together. Four
\emph{semantics} dominate the classical picture---complete, grounded,
preferred, and stable extensions.

Two extremes anchor the theory. The \emph{grounded extension} is the
least complete extension---the cautious, skeptical verdict. The
\emph{preferred extensions} are the maximal admissible sets---the boldest
self-defensible stances. This paper treats the maximal extreme, with no
finiteness and no well-foundedness hypothesis on the framework.

Our thesis is that the entire completeness-and-existence theory of
preferred extensions flows from a single lemma. The Fundamental Lemma
states that admissibility grows freely along defended arguments. Once
available: maximality forces closure under defense; chain-unions of
admissible sets are admissible; and the two facts fuse into the identity
\emph{preferred $=$ maximal complete}, yielding a pointed poset of complete
extensions.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $A$ is a set of \emph{arguments} and $R \subseteq A \times A$
is the \emph{attack relation}; we write $R\,a\,b$ for ``$a$ attacks
$b$.'' A pair $(A,R)$ is an \emph{argumentation framework}; no cardinality
or acyclicity assumption is made.

\begin{definition}[Conflict-free]
$S$ is \emph{conflict-free} if $\forall a \in S,\ \forall b \in S,\
\neg\, R\,a\,b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Defense]
$S$ \emph{defends} $a$ if $\forall b,\ R\,b\,a \Rightarrow \exists c \in
S,\ R\,c\,b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Characteristic operator]
The \emph{characteristic operator} is $F(S) = \{\, a \in A \mid S \text{
defends } a \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible]
$S$ is \emph{admissible} if it is conflict-free and $S \subseteq F(S)$
(equivalently, $S$ defends each of its members).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complete]
$S$ is a \emph{complete extension} if it is admissible and $F(S) \subseteq
S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Stable]
$S$ is a \emph{stable extension} if it is conflict-free and
$\forall a \notin S,\ \exists b \in S,\ R\,b\,a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Preferred]
$S$ is a \emph{preferred extension} if it is a maximal admissible set:
admissible, and every admissible $T \supseteq S$ equals $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Grounded]
The \emph{grounded extension} is the least complete extension under
inclusion (the least fixed point of $F$ obtained by iterating from
$\emptyset$).
\end{definition}

We shall establish the class inclusions
$\text{stable} \subseteq \text{preferred} \subseteq \text{complete}
\subseteq \text{admissible}$.

\section{Monotonicity}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of defense]\label{lem:defmono}
If $S \subseteq T$ and $S$ defends $a$, then $T$ defends $a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Any attacker $b$ of $a$ is counterattacked by some $c \in S \subseteq T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:Fmono}
$S \subseteq T$ implies $F(S) \subseteq F(T)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Fundamental Lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental Lemma]\label{thm:fund}
If $S$ is admissible and $S$ defends $a$, then $S \cup \{a\}$ is
admissible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $S' = S \cup \{a\}$.

\emph{Conflict-freedom.} Since $S$ is conflict-free, only conflicts
involving $a$ are possible.
(1) If $R\,a\,b$ for some $b \in S$, then $a$ attacks the member $b$; as
$S$ defends $b$, some $c \in S$ has $R\,c\,a$; as $S$ defends $a$, some
$d \in S$ has $R\,d\,c$, contradicting conflict-freedom of $S$.
(2) If $R\,b\,a$ for some $b \in S$, then as $S$ defends $a$, some $c \in
S$ has $R\,c\,b$, so $b,c \in S$ with $R\,c\,b$ contradicts
conflict-freedom of $S$. Hence $S'$ is conflict-free.

\emph{Defense.} Members of $S$ are defended by $S \subseteq S'$ (Lemma
\ref{lem:defmono}); and $a$ is defended by $S \subseteq S'$ by hypothesis.
Thus $S'$ is admissible.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The load-bearing content is: \emph{an admissible set can neither attack
nor be attacked by any argument it defends}. Defense and hostility are
mutually exclusive within an admissible set, which is exactly why
admissibility is a growing, rather than fragile, property.
\end{remark}

\section{Preferred extensions are complete}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:prefcomplete}
Every preferred extension is complete.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be preferred. It remains to prove $F(S) \subseteq S$. If $S$
defends $a$, then $S \cup \{a\}$ is admissible (Theorem \ref{thm:fund})
and contains $S$; maximality gives $S \cup \{a\} = S$, so $a \in S$.
\end{proof}

\section{Stable extensions}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:stablecomplete}
Every stable extension is complete (hence admissible).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be stable. If $b$ attacks $a \in S$, then $b \notin S$ (else a
conflict), so stability yields $c \in S$ with $R\,c\,b$; thus $S$ defends
$a$ and is admissible. If $a \in F(S) \setminus S$, stability gives
$b \in S$ with $R\,b\,a$, and defense gives $c \in S$ with $R\,c\,b$;
then $b,c \in S$ with $R\,c\,b$ is a conflict. Hence $F(S) \subseteq S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:stablepref}
Every stable extension is preferred.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S$ be stable, hence admissible. If $T \supseteq S$ is admissible and
$a \in T \setminus S$, stability gives $b \in S \subseteq T$ with
$R\,b\,a$, contradicting conflict-freedom of $T$. So $T = S$.
\end{proof}

\section{Existence of preferred extensions}

\begin{theorem}[Chain unions]\label{thm:chain}
If $\mathcal{S}$ is a chain of admissible sets, then $\bigcup\mathcal{S}$
is admissible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Conflict-freedom.} If $a,b \in \bigcup\mathcal{S}$ with $R\,a\,b$,
pick $S \ni a$, $T \ni b$ in $\mathcal{S}$; by the chain property both lie
in a common member, contradicting its conflict-freedom.
\emph{Defense.} If $a \in S \in \mathcal{S}$ and $b$ attacks $a$,
admissibility of $S$ gives $c \in S \subseteq \bigcup\mathcal{S}$ with
$R\,c\,b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extension]\label{thm:extend}
Every admissible set $S$ is contained in some preferred extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The poset $P_S = \{T : T \text{ admissible},\ S \subseteq T\}$ is
nonempty and, by Theorem \ref{thm:chain}, every chain in it has an upper
bound. Zorn's lemma yields a maximal $P \in P_S$, which is a maximal
admissible set containing $S$, i.e.\ a preferred extension.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]\label{thm:exists}
Every argumentation framework has a preferred extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The empty set is admissible; apply Theorem \ref{thm:extend} with
$S = \emptyset$.
\end{proof}

\section{The structural characterization}

\begin{theorem}[Preferred $=$ maximal complete]\label{thm:main}
A set $S$ is a preferred extension if and only if $S$ is a maximal
complete extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $S$ is preferred it is complete (Theorem
\ref{thm:prefcomplete}); any complete $T \supseteq S$ is admissible, so
maximality gives $T = S$.
($\Leftarrow$) If $S$ is maximal complete it is admissible; by Theorem
\ref{thm:extend} there is a preferred $P \supseteq S$, which is complete
(Theorem \ref{thm:prefcomplete}), so $S \subseteq P$ and maximality force
$P = S$; hence $S$ is preferred.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pointed poset]
Ordered by inclusion, the complete extensions of $(A,R)$ form a poset with
least element the grounded extension and maximal elements exactly the
preferred extensions, which always exist.
\end{corollary}

\section{Worked examples}

\begin{example}[Two-cycle]
$A=\{a,b\}$, $R\,a\,b$ and $R\,b\,a$. Grounded $= \emptyset$; preferred
extensions $\{a\},\{b\}$, both stable. Preferred extensions need not be
unique.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Three-cycle]
$A=\{a,b,c\}$, $R\,a\,b$, $R\,b\,c$, $R\,c\,a$. The only admissible set is
$\emptyset$: grounded $=$ preferred $= \emptyset$, and no stable extension
exists.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Defense chain]
$A=\{a,b,c\}$, $R\,b\,a$, $R\,c\,b$. Here $\{a,c\}$ is the unique
complete, preferred, and stable extension, equal to the grounded
extension: the poset collapses to a point.
\end{example}

\section{Applications}

Preferred extensions model maximally committed consistent positions in
multi-agent negotiation; complete extensions and the grounded default
structure decision support and explanation systems; stable extensions
capture fully decisive verdicts in normative reasoning. The Fundamental
Lemma gives a certified, incremental way to build justified sets of
accepted claims, a natural backbone for explainable AI.

\section{Discussion}

The development is hypothesis-free: neither finiteness nor
well-foundedness is needed. Existence follows from Theorem \ref{thm:fund},
Theorem \ref{thm:chain}, and Zorn's lemma; completeness of preferred
extensions is a two-line corollary of the Fundamental Lemma and
maximality. Routing everything through the Fundamental Lemma---rather than
bespoke fixed-point reasoning---is both shorter and generalizes cleanly to
the infinite, non-well-founded setting. Theorem \ref{thm:main} translates
a growth-flavored definition into an order-flavored one, packaging the
semantics as a pointed poset.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Complete meet-semilattice]
Every nonempty family of complete extensions has a greatest lower bound
that is again complete, computed by iterating $F$ from the intersection.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Stability dichotomy]
A framework admits a stable extension iff some preferred extension attacks
every argument outside it; equivalently, stable extensions are exactly the
preferred extensions with empty undecided region.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Topological reflection]
The nerve of the poset of complete extensions is contractible, reflecting
that the grounded extension is a canonical least element to which the
whole family retracts.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

From a single conflict-avoidance lemma we recover, without finiteness or
well-foundedness, the full maximal-extension theory of abstract
argumentation, culminating in the identity of preferred and maximal
complete extensions and the pointed-poset picture of the semantics.
Reasoning under conflict, stripped to pure structure, has the shape of an
ordered landscape.

\end{document}

